To solve for the $x$-intercept $(x_0,0)$, we substitute $y = 0$ into the given congruence:
\begin{align*}
3x &\equiv 4(0) - 1 \pmod{35} \\
3x &\equiv -1 \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
To solve for $x$, we multiply both sides by $12$, which is the modular multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $35$:
\begin{align*}
12 \cdot 3x &\equiv 12 \cdot (-1) \pmod{35} \\
36x &\equiv -12 \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
Since $36x \equiv x \pmod{35}$, we have:
\begin{align*}
x &\equiv -12 \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
To find an equivalent $x$ value in the range $0 \leq x < 35$, we add $35$ to $-12$:
\begin{align*}
x &\equiv 23 \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the $x$-intercept is at $(23,0)$.

To find the $y$-intercept $(0,y_0)$, we substitute $x = 0$ into the given congruence:
\begin{align*}
3(0) &\equiv 4y - 1 \pmod{35} \\
0 &\equiv 4y - 1 \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
Rearranging, we get:
\begin{align*}
1 &\equiv 4y \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
Multiplying both sides by $9$, which is the modular multiplicative inverse of $4$ modulo $35$:
\begin{align*}
9 \cdot 1 &\equiv 9 \cdot 4y \pmod{35} \\
9 &\equiv 36y \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
Since $36y \equiv y \pmod{35}$, we have:
\begin{align*}
y &\equiv 9 \pmod{35}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the $y$-intercept is at $(0,9)$.

Adding the values of $x_0$ and $y_0$ together:
\begin{align*}
x_0 + y_0 &= 23 + 9 \\
&= \boxed{32}.
\end{align*}
